## 1. Introduction
Enzymes are environment-friendly biocatalysts with high efficiency, specificity and suitability for reactions under mild conditions [1–3], which are extensively applied in the fields of food, medicine and biosensing [4,5]. However, as a biological macromolecule, enzymes usually suffer from various extreme factors such as pH, temperature and organic solvents in actual reactions, which are always a threat to the activity and conformation of enzymes, and thus affect the final catalytic yield [6–8]. Furthermore, it’s difficult to separate the enzyme from the product and substrate, resulting in wasted resources and high costs [9,10]. Immobilization of enzymes is an effective method to enhance the stability of enzymes [11–13]. In contrast to free enzymes, the specific performance of immobilization in changing enzyme properties includes: imparting reusability and easy separation characteristics to the enzyme; protecting the enzyme from direct exposure to harsh environments; freezing the conformation of the enzyme and increasing its rigidity; generating a special microenvironment to stabilize the enzyme structure [14]. Recently, more and more nanomaterials such as silica [15], chitosan [16] and various membranes [17] have been applied for immobilizing enzymes. Nevertheless, there are still some pressing challenges for the development of supports with controllable pore structures and surface properties to enhance the stability and activity of immobilized enzymes.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are spatially networked structures formed by the coordination of organic ligands and inorganic metal ions or metal clusters. Due to large specific surface area, tunable porosity, and structural diversity, MOFs have gained lots of attention for immobilizing enzymes [18–20]. In general, the strategies for immobilizing enzymes by MOFs are mainly divided into in-situ encapsulation, surface immobilization and pore entrapment with pre synthetic MOFs. For in-situ encapsulation, which is also classified into co-precipitation and biomineralization, enzymes are usually encapsulated inside the MOFs along with the process of MOFs synthesis, reflecting the shielding effect of MOFs on the enzyme against extreme environments [21,22]. For surface immobilization, enzymes are immobilized onto the surface of MOFs using covalent bonds or physical absorption, which is applicable to the synthesis of MOFs under harsh conditions and enzymes with large molecular substrates [23,24]. For pore entrapment with pre synthetic MOFs, enzymes are dispersed into the pore interior of the pre synthetic MOFs, MOFs can provide a microenvironmental protective effect preventing enzyme aggregation and unfolding [25]. However, these immobilization strategies are still facing significant limitations. For example, the structure of the enzyme may be destroyed due to the formation of covalent bonds between enzymes and MOFs; enzyme leaching may occur by physical adsorption; and coprecipitation/biomineralization could cause undesired enzyme conformational change. In particular, the intrinsic open channels of most reported MOFs are generally smaller than $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which significantly prevents the passage of enzymes, and the diffusion efficiency of substrates and products [26]. Therefore, the development of new MOFs with hierarchically porous structure is highly desirable for achieving highly efficient immobilization enzymes.
In recent years, some efforts have been made to synthesize hierarchical micro- and mesoporous MOFs (HMOFs) [27,28]. HMOFs not only inherit the porosity and large specific surface area of MOFs, but also possess more mesopores which provided enough space for enzyme molecules and catalytic reactions [29]. Meanwhile, the presence of micropores also reduces mass transfer limitation for substrates and products [30], leading to excellent catalytic efficiency. In particular, HMOFs with core–shell nanostructures have been ideal carriers for immobilization enzymes due to large void space and specific surface area [31,32]. Generally, strategies for the synthesis of HMOFs include ligand-extension, mixed ligand, defect-formation strategy and template-assisted synthesis [26,33,34]. For instance, Wu et al. chose nucleotide and 2-methylimidazole (2-Melm) to compete for zinc ions, and obtained enzyme@ZIFs composites with mesoporous structure by regulating the quantitative ratios between the two ligands [35]. Hu et al. employed a microfluidic system to regulate the concentration of the precursors zinc ion and 2-Melm, which resulted in a coordination deficiency and the emergence of a mesoporous structure, while embedded the enzyme into the interior of the mesopore [36]. Cheng et al. prepared hydrogels as templates and encapsulated them into the interior of ZIF-8, and then used high temperature to remove the hydrogels to form the mesoporous structure of HZIF-8 for enzyme immobilization [33]. Although substantial research efforts have been made, the synthesis of hierarchical porous MOFs for immobilization enzymes, especially HMOFs with core–shell superstructure still remains extremely challenging.
Co-immobilization of multi-enzyme systems is an effective method for multi-step synthesis of enzymatic reaction processes and has experienced rapid growth in terms of preparation of specific products and biosensors [37]. Compared to single enzyme immobilization, co-immobilization allows the combination of multiple enzymes and exhibits several advantages: avoiding instability due to prolonged diffusion of intermediates; accelerating the reaction rate due to elimination of lag time; and reducing the inhibition effect of high substrate concentration in the next enzyme [38,39]. Glucose oxidase (GOx) is able to oxidize glucose into gluconic acid and hydrogen peroxide $\left(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}\right)$ , and the chromogenic agent is oxidized by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to develop colored product in the presence of peroxidase, which is a common glucose detection method [40]. Besides, the production of gluconic acid by immobilized GOx has great potential for industrial applications [41]. Here, we report a new, green, and easily manageable soft templating strategy for the preparation of hierarchical micro-mesoporous zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) with core–shell superstructure (HZIF-8) for enzyme immobilization. Cytochrome C (Cyt C), a small molecule protein consisting of 104 amino acids that possesses antioxidant properties and peroxidase activity [42–45], has been chosen as model enzyme for the formation of immobilized enzyme (Cyt C@HZIF-8). The hydrated zinc sulfate $(Z\mathrm{n}\mathrm{S}0_{4}.7\mathrm{H}_{2}0);$ forms an aggregation of colloids due to the self-conglobation effect in methanol [46], which affords a soft template for the formation of HZIF-8. Meanwhile, Cyt C was entrapped into the interior of the HZIF-8 in one step accompanied by the formation of HZIF-8. The resultant Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ displayed higher catalytic activity than the free Cyt C and Cyt C encapsulated in conventional microporous ZIF-8 (Cyt C@ZIF-8). As the proof of concept, we co-immobilized two enzymes, GOx and Cyt C, and demonstrated that the coimmobilized enzymes within HZIF-8 (Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx) exhibited good sensitivity and selectivity for detection of glucose.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
2-methylimidazole $98\%$ , 2-Melm), zinc sulfate heptahydrate ( $99\%$ , $\mathrm{ZnSO}_{4}{\cdot}7\mathrm{H}_{2}0\mathrm{\cdot}$ ), $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -azino-bis(3-ethylbenzothiazoline-6-sulfo nic acid) $98\%$ , ABTS) were purchased from Aladdin Reagent Inc. (Shanghai, China). Cytochrome C (BR, EC 1.9.3.1 from horse heart, Cyt C), Glucose oxidase (BR, EC 1.1.3.4, $100{-}250~\mathrm{u/mg}$ GOx), Rhodamine B isothiocyanate $90\%$ RhB) and Fluorescein isothiocynate ( $90\%$ , FITC) were obtained from yuanye bio-technology Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). D-Glucose $(99.8\%)$ was purchased from solarbio science & technology co., Ltd (Beijing, China). All the other chemicals were of analytical grade.
## 2.2. Synthesis of HZIF-8, Cyt C@HZIF-8, Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx
HZIF-8 was prepared according to the previous work by Singh et al with a minor modification [46]. Firstly, $0.144\mathrm{~g~}\mathrm{ZnSO_{4}}\cdot7\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $0.411\mathrm{\g}2$ -Melm were dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ anhydrous methanol, respectively, followed by mixing and stirring for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then the precipitate was centrifuged and soaked with water for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and finally washed with deionized water for three times to obtain HZIF-8. For Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ , except for the addition of Cyt $(1.5~\mathrm{mg}/\$ mL), other preparation conditions were consistent with those of HZIF-8. For Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx, the resultant Cyt C@HZIF-8 was suspended in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathtt{p H8.0}$ Tris-HCl buffer ( $10~\mathrm{mM}$ , pH 8.0) containing 5 mg GOx and stir for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Then the precipitate was centrifuged and washed three times with deionized water to obtain Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx.
## 2.3. Synthesis of ZIF-8, Cyt C@ZIF-8 and Cyt C@ZIF-8/GOx
ZIF-8 was synthesized by the methods of Zhu et al [47]. Briefly, 2-Melm and $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ were dissolved in methanol with a final concentration of $25~\mathrm{mM}$ , and sonicated for $25{\mathrm{~min}}$ . Then the mixture was kept stationary for $24\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature. Finally, the precipitate was centrifuged and washed three times with methanol, and dried under vacuum for $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ to obtain ZIF-8. For Cyt $\mathsf{C@Z I F{-}8}$ , except for the addition of Cyt C $(2.5~\mathrm{mg/mL},1~\mathrm{mL})$ , other preparation conditions were consistent with those of ZIF-8. For Cyt C@ZIF-8/GOx, the preparation conditions were consistent with those of Cyt $\mathsf{C@H Z I F-8/G O x}.$
## 2.4. Enzymatic activity assay
For Cyt C, the reaction system consisted of $760~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ ( $10~\mathrm{mM}$ , pH 8.0) Tris-HCl, $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $10~\mathrm{mM}$ ) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ $3.64~\mathrm{mM}^{\cdot}$ ABTS and $40~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $0.5~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ protein) Cyt C or immobilized Cyt C. After reacting for $2\ \mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, the concentration of oxidized ABTS was determined by measuring the increasing absorbance value at $415\ \mathrm{nm}$ . One unit of Cyt C activity was defined as the amount of enzyme required to oxidize $1~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ of ABTS per min.
For colorimetric detection of glucose, the reaction system consisted of ${800}~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ $50~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ GO/Tris-HCl solution, $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ $\mathsf{5.46~m M},$ 1 ABTS and immobilized GOx/Cyt C. After reacting for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, the oxidized ABTS concentration was determined by measuring the increasing absorbance value at $415~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
## 2.5. Measurement of kinetic parameters
For Cyt C, the kinetic parameters were determined by detecting the initial reaction rates for different $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations. In detail, the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the reaction system was adjusted from 1 to $50~\mathrm{mM}$ and the reactions were performed separately to measure the enzyme activity following the activity assay method. For multiple enzymes, the kinetic parameters were obtained by adjusting the glucose concentration and measured activity following the activity assay method. All the kinetic parameters were calculated according to the Michaelis-Menten equation.
## 2.6. Synthesis of RhB or FITC labeled proteins
## 2.7. Reusability
The reusability of immobilized enzyme was tested by repetitive measurements of activity. After each measurement of activity, the immobilized enzyme was recovered and washed with $10~\mathrm{mM}$ TrisHCl $\left(\mathsf{p H8}.0\right)$ solution and then added to the next cycle.
## 2.8. Characterization
Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were taken on Sigma 300 at a voltage of $3~\mathrm{kV}.$ Transmission electron microscope (TEM) and Energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) were performed on Talos G2 200X at an operating voltage of $200\mathrm{kV}.$ . Laser scanning confocal microscopy (CLSM) was used to detect fluorescence with the type of Olympus FV1000. Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed using TA instruments Waters LLC (Q50) with a warming rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ from room temperature to $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under nitrogen environment. Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra was recorded by NICO-LET IS50 with KBr as background. X-ray diffraction (XRD) was conducted by UlTIMA IV at a voltage of $40\mathrm{kV}$ with Cu Ka anode $\mathrm{^{\prime}k}=0.15406\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{^{\prime}}$ , and diffraction patterns were obtained from 5 to $55^{\circ}$ with a scanning rate of $5^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ . The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption analysis was acquired at 77.35 K with Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET, AUTOSORB IQ), and the outgas conditions were maintained at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The surface area, pore volume, micropores and mesopores distribution were calculated by the methods of BET, v-t, BJH, HK and DFT models. UV spectrum was taken on Shimadzu UV-2550 PC. Fluorescence analysis was taken by HITACHI F-7100 fluorescence spectroscope. Circular dichroism (CD) spectra were carried out on New MOS-450.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterization of immobilized enzyme
A brief self-assembly process of the immobilized enzyme was illustrated in Fig. 1. Firstly, Cyt C and $\mathrm{ZnSO_{4}{\cdot}7H_{2}O}$ colloid spheres were uniformly dispersed in methanol solution, and then 2-Melm was added to directly coordinate with zinc to form a shell consisting of small ZIF-8 nanoparticles on the surface of $\mathrm{ZnSO_{4}{\cdot}7H_{2}O}$ colloid spheres. When the $\mathrm{ZnSO_{4}{\cdot}7H_{2}O}$ colloid spheres with ZIF-8 shell was converted to the aqueous phase, the $\mathrm{ZnSO_{4}{\cdot}7H_{2}O}$ colloid was dissolved to obtain spherical shaped HZIF-8. Meanwhile, Cyt C was immobilized in interior of HZIF-8 by entrapment. To confirm co-immobilization of enzyme proteins with different sizes, GOx was further immobilized on the surface of Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ by adsorption. TEM images confirmed that $\mathrm{ZnSO_{4}{\cdot}7H_{2}O}$ colloids exhibited homogeneous spheres morphology (Fig. 2a and b). Each of these spheres was a sparse structure, formed by the selfagglomeration effect of zinc sulfate $\left(\mathrm{{ZnSO_{4}}}\right)$ particles in methanol. SEM images revealed that HZIF-8 were well-defined spherical particles with size ranging from 0.5 to $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , and the surface was composed of small ZIF-8 nanocrystals, typically of $20{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ in size (Fig. 2c, Fig. S1a). HZIF-8 with core–shell structure can be observed by TEM (Fig. 2d). Multilayer ZIF-8 crystals formed the shell with a thickness of $50\mathrm{nm}$ . The inner cavity of the HZIF-8 contained lumps of the ZIF-8 nanocrystals, and possessed a mesoporous structure with distinct channels. After immobilizing Cyt C and GOx, no significant morphological changes were observed for the HZIF-8 (Fig. 2e, 2f, 2g, 2h, Fig. S1b and S1c).
To confirm the presence of Cyt C and GOx molecules in the HZIF8, the EDS analysis was used and the results were shown in Fig. 3 and Figure S2. The C, N, S and Zn elements can be observed in HZIF-8, Cyt C @HZIF-8, and Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx, which prove the formation of ZIF-8. Also, Cyt C is a ferric enzyme, which contains a Fe atom in the active center [44]. The Fe elements on Cyt C $:\textcircled{\sc}\mathrm{HZIF}{-8}$ , and Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx were clearly observed, which prove the presence of Cyt C molecules in the HZIF-8. The results of CLSM revealed that HZIF-8 with Cyt C only emitted red fluorescence (RhB label) (Fig. 4a), HZIF-8 with GOx only displayed green fluorescence (FITC label) (Fig. 4b), which was only present on the outer shell layer and surface of the carrier. However, HZIF-8 with Cyt C and GOx yielded two kinds of fluorescence (Fig. 4c), where the green fluorescence was on the surface and shell layer of the carrier, while the red fluorescence was in the interior. This result confirmed the presence of both RhB–Cyt C and FITC–GOx in Cyt $\mathsf{C@H Z I F-8/G O x}.$ Furthermore, TGA results showed that the main weight loss of Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ and Cyt $\mathsf{C@H Z I F-8/G O x}$ occurred from $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ can be due to thermal degradation of the enzyme protein molecules (Fig. 4d), further proving the presence of Cyt C and GOx. Combined the results of EDS, CLSM and TGA, Cyt C and GOx can be immobilized successfully through the proposed method in this work.
The characteristic peaks at 421, 1310 and $1420~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in ZIF-8, Cyt $\mathsf{C@Z I F{-}8}$ , HZIF-8, Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ and Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx were ascribed to the $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ stretching and imidazole ring stretching vibrations (Fig. 5a) [6], indicating the formation of ZIF-8. The peaks at $1650~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in Cyt C, GOx, Cyt C@ZIF-8, Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ and Cyt $\mathsf{C@H Z I F-8/G O x}$ were corresponded to the amide I band, demonstrating the presence of enzyme proteins [47]. The results of XRD confirmed that HZIF-8 exhibited high crystallinity and remained consistent with the peak pattern of conventional ZIF-8. Also, the Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ and Cyt $\mathsf{C@H Z I F-8/G O x}$ displayed the same diffraction pattern with conventional ZIF-8 (Fig. 5b), indicating that the presence of enzyme proteins didn’t cause changes in HZIF-8 crystalline structure.
In addition, $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm exhibited that the conventional ZIF-8 showed a type I isotherm, indicating microporous structure [48]. However, HZIF-8 displayed a type IV isotherm with a significant hysteresis loop at high relative pressure (Fig. 5c), suggesting the existence of mesoporous [49]. The surface mesoporosity can arise from the interstices between the small ZIF8 crystals comprising the sphere shell wall. After pore size calculation by DFT method, the pore size distribution curve of HZIF-8 in the micropore interval matched that of ZIF-8, while a major mesopore distribution concentrated at $8.5\mathrm{nm}$ in HZIF-8 can be observed (Fig. 5d). Furthermore, the pore volume increased from 0.593 (ZIF8) to $0.909\ \mathrm{cc/g}$ (HZIF-8) (Table 1). BJH analysis showed that the average pore size of HZIF-8 is $11.497~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which was higher than that of ZIF-8, indicating the presence of micropores and mesopores in HZIF-8. The hierarchical micro-and mesoporous ZIF-8 with core– shell superstructures could be used as suitable carriers for enzyme immobilization.
## 3.2. Catalytic performance of Cyt C@HZIF-8
The activity of free Cyt C, Cyt C@ZIF-8, and Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ was compared. The results were shown in Fig. 6a, it was found that a significant increase in the activity when Cyt C was immobilized in the HZIF-8, where the activity of Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ was nearly 4- fold and 3-fold higher than free Cyt C and Cyt $C@Z I F-8$ , respectively. And both supports had no effects on the assay of activity. Meanwhile, the immobilization yield of Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ showed $85\%$ , which was lower than Cyt C@ZIF-8 $(90\%)$ (Table. S1). The decreased immobilization yield could due to leakage of enzyme proteins during template removal. However, the expressed activity of Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ was still maintained about 3 times higher than that of Cyt C@ZIF-8. In order to explore reasons for the increase of activity, the effects of medium including methanol, $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , and 2-Melm on Cyt C were investigated. As shown in Fig. 6b, the Cyt C activity in methanol and 2-Melm increased 1.38-fold and 1.34 fold higher than that of Cyt C in water. However, the presence of zinc ions caused a decrease of the activity. The UV spectrum indicated that the imidazole affects the native structure of Cyt C (Fig. 6c). To confirm the changes of Cyt C structure, the secondary structure of Cyt C was further determined by CD spectrum and the results were summarized in Table. 2. The $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ -helix of Cyt C in imidazole displayed a decrease in comparison to the native Cyt C, suggesting that 2-Melm induces changes in the conformation of Cyt C. And after immobilizing with HZIF-8, the results of secondary structure calculated by FITR showed in Table. S2. The $\mathsf{\Omega}\propto$ -helix of Cyt C still maintained the same trend as Table. 2. The changes of Cyt C conformation might cause exposure of active sites of Cyt C and the increasing of ${\mathfrak{\beta}}\qquad$ -sheet can offer more hydrogen bonds, which can facilitate the contact reaction with the substrate, thus enhancing the enzyme activity [50,51].
Besides, Kinetic parameters were obtained by fitting a nonlinear curve between immobilized enzymes and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentration (Fig. 6d). Although the $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ values of both Cyt C@HZIF-8 and Cyt C@ZIF-8 were higher than free Cyt C, the $\sf{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ of Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ was 2.75-fold lower than Cyt ${\mathsf{C@Z I F}}{\boldsymbol{-8}}$ (Table 3), indicating that the affinity of Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ for the substrate was significantly stronger than Cyt C@ZIF-8. The enhanced affinity of Cyt C@HZIF8 for the substrate are attributing to the hierarchical micro-and mesoporous structure of HZIF-8 that not onl improves mass transfer transport, but also provides a good microenvironment for the enzyme [52]. Also, the conformational changes of enzymes, diffusional effects of substrate and steric hindrance may result in the variation of $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ [53,54]. In addition, Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ and Cyt $C@$ ZIF-8 exhibited higher $\mathtt{V}_{\mathrm{max}}$ values than free Cyt C. The enhanced $\mathtt{V}_{\mathtt{m a x}}$ could be due to the interaction between the immobilized Cyt C and imidazole in ZIF-8 crystals to the enhancement of the catalytic activity of Cyt C, which can be observed when incubating free Cyt C with free imidazole in solution (Fig. 6b). Moreover, the reusability of Cyt $C@\mathrm{HZIF}{-}8$ was determined and results were showed in Fig. 7a. After seven repetitions, Cyt C@HZIF-8 could still maintain $70\%$ of its initial activity, whereas Cyt $C@Z I F-8$ only retained $11\%$ of its initial activity, indicating that CytC $@$ HZIF-8 has better reusability than conventional Cyt C@ZIF-8.
## 3.3. Performance of glucose detection
Ideally, HZIF-8 with hierarchical pore structure is beneficial to the compartmentalization of the multienzyme [55]. Cyt C with peroxidase activity enables a direct cascade reaction with GOx. To demonstrate the effectiveness of the HZIF-8 for multienzyme immobilization, Cyt C and GOx were co-immobilized within HZIF-8 for glucose detection by colorimetric assay. The results showed that Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx was able to detect glucose concentration, and exhibited good linearity to the glucose concentrations in the range from $10~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $50~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (Fig. 7c and 7b). The detection limit of the Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx was estimated at $0.8~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and the response time was only about $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . Comparation for glucose detection between Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx and some previous reported biocatalysts were given in Table. S3. For example, Lin et al. prepared a novel glucose oxidase@Cu-hemin metal–organic framework as a bioenzyme catalyst for glucose detection with a detection limit of $2.8~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and a reaction time of $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ [56]. Sun et al. developed the self–assembled nanoparticles of 3, 30, 5, 50 tetramethylbenzidine (TMB), glucose oxidase and horseradish peroxidase (TMB-GH NPS) that incubated with glucose for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , and the calculated detection limit was $12\upmu\mathrm{M}$ [57]. In comparision, the Cyt $\mathsf{C@H Z I F-8/G O x}$ in this study exhibited lower detection limit and reaction time. Furthermore, the selectivity and anti-interference ability of Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx were assessed by detecting the absorbance values (A415) in the presence of different interferents such as lactose, maltose, mannose, galactose and fructose. Although the concentrations of lactose, maltose, mannose, galactose and fructose were 10-fold higher than that of glucose, no distinct signal interference was observed (Fig. 7d), indicating that Cyt ${\mathsf{C@H Z I F-8/}}$ GOx possesses a specific response to glucose. In addition, by measuring the kinetic parameters of double enzymes (Table. S4), it could be found that the $\mathtt{V}_{\mathrm{max}}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ values of both Cyt ${\cal C}@2\mathrm{IF}{-}8/$ GOx and Cyt $\mathsf{C@H Z I F-8/G O x}$ were higher and lower than Cyt $C/$ GOx, which can be explained that coimmobilization of multiple enzymes can shorten the distance of intermediate product transport and reduce lag time [39]. Meanwhile, the Km value of Cyt $\mathsf{C@H Z I F-8/G O x}$ was lower than conventional Cyt $\mathsf{C}@2\mathrm{IF}{-}8/\mathsf{G}0\mathrm{x}.$ The reason might be that the mesoporous structure is more conducive to the transport diffusion of intermediates.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, based on previous methods [46], a facile hierarchical micro-mesoporous ZIF-8 with core–shell superstructure by using colloidal $Z_{\mathrm{{nSO}_{4}}}$ as a soft template to construct multienzyme system was proposed in this work. It has been demonstrated that HZIF-8 owns superior properties as supports for enzyme immobilization and the obtained catalysts can be applied as bio-sensor of glucose. Compared with conventional Cyt C@ZIF8, the Cyt C@HZIF-8 exhibited remarkably enhanced enzymatic activity and improved operational stability. The hierarchical micro-mesoporous structure displayed a better mass transfer improvement, achieving similar results compared to some reports [33,58]. Also, Cyt C@HZIF-8/GOx showed a lower LOD and high selectivity for glucose. To our knowledge, this is the first time that multiple enzymes were simultaneously immobilized into hierarchical micro-mesoporous ZIF-8 with zinc sulfate as a soft template using entrapment and adsorption methods. Thus, this strategy will not only help us to design novel hierarchical porous MOF with core–shell superstructure, but also promote the biotechnological application of MOFs materials.
